d it  because i just wanted to be sure that i  weren't going to do any damage to  anybody  what's been the hardest thing for you  since your diagnosis  it's not feeling really good  and it has changed my life as far as  friendships and things like that and  going out and  going on holiday with friends and  um i think when you've got dementia  people step back because they're they're  more afraid of it than what you are  yeah so they're  which is really sad to hear and what  about your own confidence  in your ability to to do those things  um i think i'm quite confident i  i think i mean i could go on bragging  about myself but  i'll ask you you're very welcome how  about you get your daughter to do the  bragging for you  my mum is really confident and she's  she's quite an upbeat type of person  anyway  so she doesn't particularly get um  anxious about the condition she's a very  positive person  and i think that's helped my mum quite a  lot  um but there has been days when it has  upset her  because she knows things are changing  and things are deteriorating and she  can't quite  work things out quite as easily as what  she could and needs  prompted so that has had an effect on  her well your positivity  really does shine through sylvia let me  just bring in uh professor paul morgan  here  to give us a little bit more about the  potential of  this new drug there are very few  treatments and  no new drugs for almost two decades i  believe how significant a step forward  could this be  really sorry i've got a phone call keeps  coming through as you were talking then  i only caught part of what you said  could you repeat the question  yes i was just asking how significant a  step forward this new drug could be  for the treatment of alzheimer's  well i think it's it's unfortunate that  i have to say that we don't really  know this has been a very controversial  drug it's been around in development and  in  trials for a few years now and its  successes have been very li